    <div class="max-w-4xl">
      <p class="text-gray-600 mb-6">Thymosin Beta-4 is a naturally occurring 43-amino acid polypeptide found in virtually all mammalian cells. It was originally identified as the thymic hormone responsible for T-cell maturation, but subsequent research revealed its primary intracellular function: G-actin sequestration. By maintaining the cellular actin monomer pool, Thymosin Beta-4 regulates cytoskeletal dynamics critical for cell migration, wound healing, and angiogenesis. Unlike its synthetic fragment TB-500, full-length Thymosin Beta-4 retains the complete N-terminal actin-binding domain and C-terminal regulatory regions, offering the full biological activity of the native protein.</p>
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold mb-3">Published Research Focus Areas</h3>
