    <div class="space-y-3 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <details class="card group cursor-pointer"><summary class="font-semibold text-primary hover:text-accent">What is the difference between TB-500 and Thymosin Beta-4?</summary><p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 text-sm">TB-500 is the synthetic N-terminal fragment (Ac-LKKTETQ, ~889 Da) of the full 43-amino acid Thymosin Beta-4 protein. TB-500 retains the core actin-binding motif but lacks the full C-terminal regulatory sequence. Thymosin Beta-4 is the complete native protein with broader biological activity including additional binding domains and post-translational interaction sites.</p></details>
      <details class="card group cursor-pointer"><summary class="font-semibold text-primary hover:text-accent">What research applications is Thymosin Beta-4 used for?</summary><p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 text-sm">Primarily wound healing, angiogenesis, cardiac protection, and tissue regeneration research. Its actin-sequestering activity makes it a key tool in cytoskeletal dynamics studies. Also investigated in dermal repair, corneal healing, and hair follicle biology.</p></details>
      <details class="card group cursor-pointer"><summary class="font-semibold text-primary hover:text-accent">How should Thymosin Beta-4 be stored and reconstituted?</summary><p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 text-sm">Reconstitute with sterile water or PBS (pH 7.0–7.4). The full-length protein is slightly more complex to handle than TB-500 due to its larger size. Aliquot single-use portions and store at -20°C. Avoid freeze-thaw cycles. Lyophilized powder: 24-month stability at 2–8°C.</p></details>
